Chapter: LexStubs

 Outside the kernel there are no phase decks; the identity stub mirrors
 the one PlugTypes.codex carries for the same reason.

 deck-short-of asks whether the deck is within a guard band of a
 ceiling. With no deck there is no ceiling, and callers here pass 0,
 for which the real definition in Core/PhaseAllocator.codex returns
 False as well. It is written to stay loud rather than convenient: a
 caller that starts passing a real ceiling gets True, which empties the
 parse and shows up as a diff, instead of a quietly optimistic False.

 copy-sx-diag arrived with Update 54 and is the SAME STUB AS deck-record,
 for the same reason and in the same expression. U54's lexer rewinds the
 bivy after every token, so a diagnostic built there is overwritten by the
 next token's scan; `lex-errors-push` copies the cell onto the deck first --
 `deck-record (__linked-list-push errs (copy-sx-diag 0 d))`. Outside the
 kernel there is no bivy to rewind and no deck to copy onto, so the copy has
 nothing to protect against and the identity is the faithful answer. Half
 that expression was already stubbed here; this is the other half.

 THE REAL DEFINITION LIVES IN Syntax/SyntaxNodes.codex AND THE LEX SLICE
 CANNOT REACH IT. Bundling that chapter was tried first and measured, because
 using real code beats writing a stub: it needs `map-list`, which needs
 `codex/foreword/core/ListUtils.codex`, and then it needs OverflowMode,
 DiagnosticBag, AnnotationNode and the three Prose node types -- six more
 chapters, at which point the lex bundle IS the parse bundle and the cheapest
 rung stops being the thin front slice it exists to be.

 WHAT THIS STUB IS NOT EXERCISED BY, said plainly. The lex subject is
 Lexer.codex lexed by itself and its truth ends `errors 0`, so no diagnostic
 is ever pushed and this function is never called on the measured path. That
 makes the two options byte-identical here and it is why the choice was safe;
 it also means a WRONG stub would not show up in this rung, which is the
 honest risk and the reason the argument above is written out rather than
 assumed. If a lex subject ever does produce an error, the identity is still
 what a hosted process wants, and the diff it would move is the lexer's own
 diagnostic bytes.

Section: Stubs
  deck-record : a -> a
  deck-record (x) = x

  copy-sx-diag : Integer, Diagnostic -> Diagnostic
  copy-sx-diag (b) (d) = d

  deck-short-of : Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  deck-short-of (ceiling) (band) =
   if ceiling > 0 then True else False
